5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86bf12b6-5a27-5431-8d29-d99f19a48a7d" :answer/id #uuid "86bf12b6-5a27-5431-8d29-d99f19a48a7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3dce9be-488e-597e-98f1-e5e643b35fa5" :question/id #uuid "e3dce9be-488e-597e-98f1-e5e643b35fa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213273" :question/problem "Given that $8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=2,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 4a≡2\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4a≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-565a5af7-cb23-5307-b611-3830540eec60" "answer-field-860799aa-563b-5765-9674-6e6717a31246" "answer-field-0df402a2-7ed9-5047-82bc-0292c8aa9b7c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-565a5af7-cb23-5307-b611-3830540eec60" :answer-field/id #uuid "565a5af7-cb23-5307-b611-3830540eec60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-be014bb4-a128-5964-8cf4-216674c291c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-871bfca8-10c7-5d91-8023-5fca31ddcf7d" "answer-be014bb4-a128-5964-8cf4-216674c291c8" "answer-8f264e4d-4d43-5d20-9fc3-ed5a13312c8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-871bfca8-10c7-5d91-8023-5fca31ddcf7d" :answer/id #uuid "871bfca8-10c7-5d91-8023-5fca31ddcf7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be014bb4-a128-5964-8cf4-216674c291c8" :answer/id #uuid "be014bb4-a128-5964-8cf4-216674c291c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f264e4d-4d43-5d20-9fc3-ed5a13312c8b" :answer/id #uuid "8f264e4d-4d43-5d20-9fc3-ed5a13312c8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-860799aa-563b-5765-9674-6e6717a31246" :answer-field/id #uuid "860799aa-563b-5765-9674-6e6717a31246" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-caaf1609-8130-5ddd-8140-7dd84fc5c06a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5babb7e-bd9e-5572-bb47-92fbf9522903" "answer-79d41087-cf96-5b44-a7d3-1934efa65193" "answer-caaf1609-8130-5ddd-8140-7dd84fc5c06a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5babb7e-bd9e-5572-bb47-92fbf9522903" :answer/id #uuid "f5babb7e-bd9e-5572-bb47-92fbf9522903" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79d41087-cf96-5b44-a7d3-1934efa65193" :answer/id #uuid "79d41087-cf96-5b44-a7d3-1934efa65193" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caaf1609-8130-5ddd-8140-7dd84fc5c06a" :answer/id #uuid "caaf1609-8130-5ddd-8140-7dd84fc5c06a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0df402a2-7ed9-5047-82bc-0292c8aa9b7c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0df402a2-7ed9-5047-82bc-0292c8aa9b7c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-bbbb765c-2c23-5e8d-965f-42ed23bbab70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbbb765c-2c23-5e8d-965f-42ed23bbab70" "answer-3d057ada-97e9-55f4-b67b-ab3c7403b66f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbbb765c-2c23-5e8d-965f-42ed23bbab70" :answer/id #uuid "bbbb765c-2c23-5e8d-965f-42ed23bbab70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d057ada-97e9-55f4-b67b-ab3c7403b66f" :answer/id #uuid "3d057ada-97e9-55f4-b67b-ab3c7403b66f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0db74dcd-d670-5d6c-b7fa-69c8b873b4a0" :question/id #uuid "0db74dcd-d670-5d6c-b7fa-69c8b873b4a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-213286" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=10$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc4a4f00-eeac-5e19-81e7-8f21d410c0e3" "answer-field-4a196bd6-fe7f-5fb1-8c2b-77609a70a373"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc4a4f00-eeac-5e19-81e7-8f21d410c0e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4a4f00-eeac-5e19-81e7-8f21d410c0e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e494e63-dbeb-5d49-9517-6889039cbebf" "answer-0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" "answer-837f43b2-0cf6-5314-9737-f6c3b2f8c816" "answer-ce855e48-d462-5bcb-99cf-ba99969f3e21" "answer-10610c54-ff51-5307-9f90-b6eabfb1e4a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e494e63-dbeb-5d49-9517-6889039cbebf" :answer/id #uuid "6e494e63-dbeb-5d49-9517-6889039cbebf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" :answer/id #uuid "0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-837f43b2-0cf6-5314-9737-f6c3b2f8c816" :answer/id #uuid "837f43b2-0cf6-5314-9737-f6c3b2f8c816" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce855e48-d462-5bcb-99cf-ba99969f3e21" :answer/id #uuid "ce855e48-d462-5bcb-99cf-ba99969f3e21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10610c54-ff51-5307-9f90-b6eabfb1e4a7" :answer/id #uuid "10610c54-ff51-5307-9f90-b6eabfb1e4a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a196bd6-fe7f-5fb1-8c2b-77609a70a373" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a196bd6-fe7f-5fb1-8c2b-77609a70a373" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29196106-11af-548e-bfc3-e6704c15ba7c" "answer-81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29196106-11af-548e-bfc3-e6704c15ba7c" :answer/id #uuid "29196106-11af-548e-bfc3-e6704c15ba7c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62" :answer/id #uuid "81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152477 :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=18$ are coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,18)=1.$ As a result, we can cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2a≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\\,⟺\\,2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $18$ are $\\text{coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10a0a6ca-8535-5d29-a558-33adedbec76b" "answer-field-451eb193-baa8-57c7-bc96-4a6c94ae27d0" "answer-field-089f9845-9ec3-5dd6-82b2-e24dceac91c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10a0a6ca-8535-5d29-a558-33adedbec76b" :answer-field/id #uuid "10a0a6ca-8535-5d29-a558-33adedbec76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" "answer-71813022-7564-5bad-a001-0b629b968f8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" :answer/id #uuid "811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71813022-7564-5bad-a001-0b629b968f8e" :answer/id #uuid "71813022-7564-5bad-a001-0b629b968f8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-451eb193-baa8-57c7-bc96-4a6c94ae27d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "451eb193-baa8-57c7-bc96-4a6c94ae27d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" "answer-da0f3a7a-8b14-53b9-8811-33d8e99b6659" "answer-b97d8b88-8c98-51c9-978a-4e3d68460458" "answer-17fc27ca-e10f-52c0-9489-6344d9f26ee7" "answer-22d78d51-9cf3-571e-ad19-abf789362266"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" :answer/id #uuid "a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da0f3a7a-8b14-53b9-8811-33d8e99b6659" :answer/id #uuid "da0f3a7a-8b14-53b9-8811-33d8e99b6659" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b97d8b88-8c98-51c9-978a-4e3d68460458" :answer/id #uuid "b97d8b88-8c98-51c9-978a-4e3d68460458" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17fc27ca-e10f-52c0-9489-6344d9f26ee7" :answer/id #uuid "17fc27ca-e10f-52c0-9489-6344d9f26ee7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d78d51-9cf3-571e-ad19-abf789362266" :answer/id #uuid "22d78d51-9cf3-571e-ad19-abf789362266" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-089f9845-9ec3-5dd6-82b2-e24dceac91c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "089f9845-9ec3-5dd6-82b2-e24dceac91c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" "answer-0d2b6db7-48a7-5c5f-8a77-2fe48526a608"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" :answer/id #uuid "3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d2b6db7-48a7-5c5f-8a77-2fe48526a608" :answer/id #uuid "0d2b6db7-48a7-5c5f-8a77-2fe48526a608" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152478 :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3a≡2\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=2$ and the modulus $n=16$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(2,16)=2.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $2$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5232d044-7444-501b-9f80-621e603355e7" "answer-field-e5ba1118-d0b5-57b8-9b1b-d30adecd4205"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5232d044-7444-501b-9f80-621e603355e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "5232d044-7444-501b-9f80-621e603355e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" "answer-2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" "answer-1bda5ed8-d8cc-50c7-9331-e4fef732089e" "answer-f383414e-73d6-529e-81fc-27a1cd96901f" "answer-5e56c4c8-f198-52b5-8616-bfa70a20388d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" :answer/id #uuid "06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" :answer/id #uuid "2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not coprime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bda5ed8-d8cc-50c7-9331-e4fef732089e" :answer/id #uuid "1bda5ed8-d8cc-50c7-9331-e4fef732089e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f383414e-73d6-529e-81fc-27a1cd96901f" :answer/id #uuid "f383414e-73d6-529e-81fc-27a1cd96901f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e56c4c8-f198-52b5-8616-bfa70a20388d" :answer/id #uuid "5e56c4c8-f198-52b5-8616-bfa70a20388d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "prime" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5ba1118-d0b5-57b8-9b1b-d30adecd4205" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ba1118-d0b5-57b8-9b1b-d30adecd4205" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6043cc0-a7de-5794-b7fd-424efd9bf2be" "answer-df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6043cc0-a7de-5794-b7fd-424efd9bf2be" :answer/id #uuid "d6043cc0-a7de-5794-b7fd-424efd9bf2be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370" :answer/id #uuid "df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153453 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for $k\\neq 0,$ we can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $k\\neq 0,$ we have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0.$ For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153452 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1.$ \n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1,$ we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n\n$$\n6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2  \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1,$ we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n\n$$\n4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153454 :tutorial/content "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn) & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,k(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,\\cancel{k}n\\,|\\,\\cancel{k}(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n).$ Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,k(a-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n,$ we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n.$ Now, using the definition again, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\,|\\,(a-b) & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152342 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
